Here's a JavaScript function `reorderAdjectives` that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function reorderAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(' ');

  // Filter out common stop words (you can customize this list)
  const stopWords = ['a', 'an', 'the'];
  const filteredWords = words.filter((word) => !stopWords.includes(word.toLowerCase()));

  // Find all adjectives in the filtered words
  const adjectives = filteredWords.filter((word) => /\b[a-zA-Z]{3,}\b/.test(word));

  // Reverse the order of adjectives and concatenate them back to the original sentence
  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse().join(' ');
  const updatedSentence = filteredWords.slice(0, filteredWords.indexOf(adjectives[0])).concat(reversedAdjectives, filteredWords.slice(filteredWords.indexOf(adjectives[0])+1)).join(' ');

  return updatedSentence;
}

// Test the function
const input = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const output = reorderAdjectives(input);
console.log(output); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

The function first splits the sentence into an array of words, filters out common stop words, finds all adjectives, reverses their order, and then concatenates them back to the original sentence with the non-adjective words in between. The updated sentence is returned as the result. You can test the function with different inputs to make sure it works correctly for your use case. Note that this implementation assumes that adjectives always come before nouns, which may not be true in all cases. If you need more advanced handling of grammar and syntax, you may want to consider using a more sophisticated natural language processing library instead.

