1. Firstly, ensure that your WordPress site is running on HTTPS and has a valid SSL certificate. This is necessary because CORS requires secure connections.

2. Next, install the `cors` middleware in your Next.js app by running the following command:

```bash
npm install cors
```

3. In your `pages/_app.js` file, import and initialize the `cors` middleware as shown below:

```javascript
import Cors from 'cors';
import express from 'express';
const app = express();

// Initialize the CORS middleware
app.use(Cors({
    origin: process.env.WORDPRESS_URL, // Set your WordPress URL here
    methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'],
}));

// ... Rest of your code here ...
```

Make sure to replace `WORDPRESS_URL` with the URL of your WordPress site. This will allow requests from that specific domain only.

4. Now, add the `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` header to your responses to enable sending cookies along with the request. Add the following line to your `pages/_app.js` file:

```javascript
app.options('*', Cors({
    optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
}));

app.get('/api/auth', (req, res) => {
    // ... Handle authentication logic ...

    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', true);
    return res.status(200).json({ success: true });
});

// ... Rest of your code here ...
```

This will handle preflight requests and set the `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` header for all routes.

5. Finally, test your application to confirm that the CORS errors have been resolved. You can now make authenticated requests from your Next.js app to your WordPress site without any issues.

Remember to always prioritize security and minimize the risk of exposing sensitive data by implementing strict access control measures.

