Inner functions, also known as nested functions or local functions, are functions defined within another function in JavaScript. They have access to the parent function's scope and can be used to improve code readability, encapsulate logic, and prevent naming collisions with global variables. Here is an example:

```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  let message = "Hello, ";

  function innerFunction() {
    return message + "world!";
  }

  return innerFunction;
}

const myFunction = outerFunction();
console.log(myFunction()); // Output: "Hello, world!"
```

Outer functions, on the other hand, refer to any function that is not nested inside another function. These functions have their own scope and can access both the global scope and the scope of any nested functions they contain. Outer functions are essential for defining reusable functions that can be called from multiple places in your codebase. Here is an example:

```javascript
function calculateTaxes(price) {
  const taxRate = 0.15;
  const taxes = price * taxRate;
  return price + taxes;
}

console.log(calculateTaxes(100)); // Output: 115
```

In this example, `calculateTaxes` is an outer function that takes a price as input, calculates the taxes based on a fixed tax rate, and returns the total amount payable. The `taxRate` variable is declared locally within the function's scope, which prevents it from interfering with any other variables with the same name elsewhere in your codebase.

In summary, inner and outer functions serve different purposes in JavaScript programming. While inner functions help improve code readability, encapsulate logic, and avoid naming conflicts, outer functions provide reusability and modularity by allowing you to define functions that can be called from multiple locations in your application.

Subject: Technical Issue with Third-Party API Integration

Dear Support Team,

We are encountering a technical issue with the integration of the XYZ API into our multi-tiered system architecture. This issue is causing errors and preventing proper functionality of our application. In this support ticket, we will provide detailed information about the issue, propose a possible solution, and provide a timeline for resolution. We will also analyze potential root causes and impact on system performance and user experience.

System Architecture:

The following table outlines the various tiers of our system architecture:

| Tier                      | Functionality                                   |
|---------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| Presentation Layer        | User interface                                  |
| Application Layer         | Business logic, data access, and API integration |
| Data Access Layer         | Database connectivity                           |
| Data Storage Layer        | Persistent storage                              |
| Network Layer            | Communication between tiers and external services |

API Integration:

Our application integrates the XYZ API through a RESTful web service that communicates with the API via HTTP requests. The following code snippet demonstrates how we make API calls from our Java application:

```java
import org.springframework.http.*;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;

public class XYZClient {
    private static final String BASE_URL = "https://api.xyz.com";
    private RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

    public ResponseEntity<Object> getData(String path) {
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.set("Authorization", "Bearer " + ACCESS_TOKEN);
        headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
        return restTemplate.exchange(BASE_URL + path, HttpMethod.GET, null, Object.class, headers);
    }
}
```

Issue Description:

When making API calls using the `getData()` method, we are encountering the following error message:

```json
{
  "error": "Invalid token"
}
```

The `ACCESS_TOKEN` used to authorize the API call appears to have expired

