It's possible that the server's use of `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (which allows cross-origin requests from any origin) is causing your issue. When making a preflight request (an OPTIONS request sent by the browser before sending the actual request), some browsers may not include the `Authorization` header if they detect that `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` is set to `*`. This is done as a security measure to prevent unauthorized access to protected resources.

To include the `Authorization` header, you can make sure that the `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` header is also set to `true` on the server. This will allow the browser to send the `Authorization` header and credentials (such as cookies) along with the request. Here's an example of how to configure this in Express.js:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', true);
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Authorization');
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS');
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', '*');
  next();
});

// ...
```

In this example, we're setting `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` to `true`, `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` to `Authorization`, and `Access-Control-Allow-Methods` to the HTTP methods allowed for CORS. We're still allowing requests from any origin (`*`) for simplicity, but you could replace `*` with a specific domain or pattern instead.

Make sure to test your application with a client that supports CORS, such as Postman or a modern browser, to ensure that the `Authorization` header is being included correctly.

